1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,052
Great ideas need the right people. CollabHub helps students, developers, designers, and
makers find collaborators and build together.

2
00:00:08,282 --> 00:00:15,216
Instead of spreading recruitment, tasks, and conversations across different tools, the
platform brings them into one connected workflow.

3
00:00:15,446 --> 00:00:22,667
This walkthrough uses the working local application and isolated demonstration data. The
public application link appears at the end.

4
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,733
We begin as Koushik, a project owner. He creates an account and completes a short
onboarding form.

5
00:00:29,963 --> 00:00:36,010
Skills, interests, experience, and availability help the platform connect people with
suitable projects.

6
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:45,166
Profiles also provide space for a college, a biography, goals, and portfolio links.
Sessions keep account-specific information behind authenticated access.

7
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:58,211
The dashboard becomes the starting point for projects, applications, invitations, and
recent activity.

8
00:00:58,441 --> 00:01:05,854
Recommendations use transparent matching rules to compare skills, interests, availability,
experience, and collaboration preferences.

9
00:01:06,084 --> 00:01:13,654
Matching reasons help people understand each suggestion. New accounts begin with genuine
empty states and database-backed community content.

10
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:20,889
Koushik starts CampusConnect, a campus community project that needs a frontend developer
and a designer.

11
00:01:21,119 --> 00:01:27,301
He describes the idea, chooses its category and experience level, and sets the expected
duration and team size.

12
00:01:27,531 --> 00:01:35,894
Each recruiting role has its own required skills and number of openings. After publishing,
the project becomes discoverable and gets a private owner workspace.

13
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:52,336
We now switch to Maya, a collaborator looking for a React project. She searches the
project directory and opens CampusConnect.

14
00:01:52,566 --> 00:02:00,579
The detail page explains the goal, team, required skills, and available roles. She saves
the project and applies for the frontend opening.

15
00:02:00,809 --> 00:02:05,530
Her introduction and motivation become a real application, with its status visible in her
account.

16
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:16,031
Back in the owner's workspace, Koushik can review Maya's application and accept or reject
it.

17
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:25,072
Acceptance updates the role opening and team membership together in a database
transaction. This protects team capacity when requests happen at the same time.

18
00:02:25,302 --> 00:02:29,888
Maya now appears on the team and gains access to the project's private workspace.

19
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:38,782
Inside the workspace, the team can create tasks with an assignee, priority, due date, and
status.

20
00:02:39,012 --> 00:02:46,593
Koushik assigns the first frontend task to Maya. The Kanban board organizes work into To
do, In progress, Review, and Completed.

21
00:02:46,823 --> 00:02:54,010
Moving the task to Review changes its stored state. Reloading the page confirms that the
progress survives beyond the current browser view.

22
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:04,018
The Resources tab keeps useful links in the project instead of losing them in a chat
thread.

23
00:03:04,248 --> 00:03:10,086
Koushik adds the repository link, while the activity history records important project and
task changes.

24
00:03:10,316 --> 00:03:14,763
These shared workspace sections remain available to authorized members of the team.

25
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:25,817
The project discussion connects the team directly inside its workspace. Messages are
saved, and Socket.IO provides live delivery and typing indicators.

26
00:03:26,047 --> 00:03:32,971
Koushik welcomes Maya. From her separate account, Maya types and replies, and her response
arrives in the owner's conversation.

27
00:03:33,201 --> 00:03:37,108
The conversation stays attached to the project, alongside the work it supports.

28
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:49,189
Collaboration can also begin with a person. The people directory and public profiles show
skills, interests, and previous project work.

29
00:03:49,419 --> 00:03:55,742
An owner can invite a suitable collaborator to an open project role. Here, Koushik sends a
designer an invitation.

30
00:03:55,972 --> 00:04:02,671
He can also start a private direct conversation with Maya. Her reply arrives live,
independently of the team discussion.

31
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:16,177
Maya's notifications bring together application decisions, invitations, messages, and task
assignments.

32
00:04:16,407 --> 00:04:20,075
She can mark them as read and return to projects she saved earlier.

33
00:04:20,305 --> 00:04:25,873
Her dashboard now reflects real collaboration activity, rather than a fixed collection of
sample cards.

34
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:35,417
Finally, Maya logs out. Protected pages return her to sign-in, while project data and
message history remain stored.

35
00:04:35,647 --> 00:04:42,037
CollabHub combines a React interface, an Express API, MongoDB persistence, and Socket.IO
communication.

36
00:04:42,267 --> 00:04:47,456
From finding a project to recruiting a team and doing the work, the core workflows connect
end to end.
